<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gyeongsun of Silla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/927">927</a>–<a href="/years/935">935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gyeongsun was the last king of Silla, placed on the throne in 927 by the rebel leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeon-hwon">Gyeon Hwon</a> of Later Baekje after the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>. Ruling a kingdom reduced to its capital region, in 935 he surrendered peacefully to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Wang Geon</a> of Goryeo, who gave him high rank and absorbed Silla's aristocracy. He died in 978.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gyeongsun-of-silla.jpg" alt="Gyeongsun of Silla" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A painter in Goryeo dynasty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_portait_of_Gyeongsun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gyeongsun was born in 897 with the personal name Kim Pu. A sixth-generation descendant of King Munseong, he was the son of Kim Hyo-jong and of Princess Gyea, a daughter of King Heongang. His wife was Lady Jukbang of the Juksan Park clan; his eldest son was Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> and his youngest Beomgong.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Later Baekje forces sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> in 927 and forced his predecessor to kill himself, and the Later Baekje king placed Gyeongsun on the throne in order to break Silla's alliance with Goryeo. The kingdom was already gravely weakened, and Gyeongsun ruled over a small remnant of its former territory until 935, when he abdicated in favour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taejo-of-goryeo">Taejo of Goryeo</a>. He led his officials to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Songak</a> for the ceremony at the head of a convoy of treasures and women said to stretch for more than thirty li, and Taejo waited for him in the suburbs of his capital.</p><p>Taejo sealed the submission with marriages. He gave Gyeongsun his eldest daughter, Princess Nangrang, as a wife and appointed him sasim-gwan, or inspector-general, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>, the first holder of what became Goryeo's sasim-gwan system. Taejo in turn married a daughter of Gyeongsun's uncle, who became one of his queens and later the grandmother of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyeonjong-of-goryeo">Hyeonjong of Goryeo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Gyeongsun spent the rest of his life near the Goryeo capital, modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaesong</a>, and died in 978. His tomb lies in Jangnam-myeon, Yeoncheon County, in Gyeonggi Province, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a>. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samguk-sagi">Samguk sagi</a>, Crown Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a> objected to his father's submission and withdrew to Mount Kumgang as a hermit. In Korean shamanism Gyeongsun is worshipped as one of the Wangshin, or king gods, and he has been portrayed in the KBS television series Taejo Wang Geon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gyeongsun of Silla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Silla's last king, <b>Gyeongsun</b>, surrendered peacefully in 935; in 936 Taejo defeated Later Baekje at the Battle of Illicheon, reuniting the peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 935 King <b>Gyeongsun</b> surrendered his kingdom peacefully to Goryeo, which absorbed the Silla aristocracy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/silla">Silla · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
